    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The large volume of dolomite cement contained within a fault zone exposed at Jalama Beach, Santa Barbara County, CA, suggests that expulsion of basinal brine and concurrent hydrocarbon migration in the Monterey Formation is highly focused along faults. Mass balance calculations of the fluid volume required for precipitation of the observed volume of cement indicate a minimum distance of radial flow into the fault zone of 3km. Based on the strontium isotopic composition of fault cement, the distance of cross-stratigraphic, upward fluid flow corresponds to the thickness of the Monterey Formation of about 700m. This fault zone was thus highly effective in drawing fluid from the formation, with a ratio of lateral versus cross-stratigraphic flow distance in excess of 5. The observed asymmetry of the fault and associated fracture systems as well as the internal structure of the fault zone, consisting of overlapping fault segments and dilational zones, indicate that fault permeability was structurally controlled. Dilational zones within the faults and connected veins provided a preferred fluid flow conduit across the stratigraphy. Bedding-confined fractures in the country rock were likely pathways for the bedding-parallel migration of formation fluid into the fault zone. Cyclic changes in composition of fault and vein cement suggest that basinal fluid expulsion and hydrocarbon migration in the Monterey Formation is controlled by seismic fault slip, opening fluid pathways within the fault zone and connected fractures which may then become sealed during interseismic intervals.",
